ARM: OMAP2+: omap_device: don't idle a force-suspended device again

_od_suspend_noirq() calls the driver's noirq suspend and then, if the
device is not runtime suspended, idles it itself. A driver that uses
pm_runtime_force_suspend() there (i2c-omap) has idled the device already
through the omap_device runtime callbacks; if it holds a usage count
(i2c-omap takes one in .suspend so the bus stays usable until noirq),
pm_runtime_force_suspend() leaves the status RPM_ACTIVE and sets
power.needs_force_resume. The device was then idled twice ("omap_device:
omap_device_idle() called from invalid state 2") and, on resume, enabled
twice, failing resume_noirq with -EINVAL: on the N800/N810 both I2C
controllers were dead after suspend to RAM. Skip such devices.

--- a/arch/arm/mach-omap2/omap_device.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-omap2/omap_device.c
@@ -402,7 +402,13 @@
 
 	ret = pm_generic_suspend_noirq(dev);
 
-	if (!ret && !pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev)) {
+	/*
+	 * A driver's pm_runtime_force_suspend() (noirq) has idled the
+	 * device through our runtime callbacks already, but leaves it
+	 * RPM_ACTIVE with needs_force_resume while it holds a usage count.
+	 */
+	if (!ret && !pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev) &&
+	    !dev->power.needs_force_resume) {
 		if (pm_generic_runtime_suspend(dev) == 0) {
 			omap_device_idle(pdev);
 			od->flags |= OMAP_DEVICE_SUSPENDED;
